"""## Task 2: Model Building

In this task, you will implement a `BackgroundSeparater` model for pet foreground segmentation. Your goal is to implement a compact CNN by inheriting from `tf.keras.Model`. This keeps the model definition clean and makes the architecture explicit.

Unlike image classification, the model **should not** output one class probability vector for the whole image. Instead, it should output a **foreground probability mask** with shape `(batch_size, 128, 128, 1)`.

---
**How Keras subclassing works**

`BackgroundSeparater(tf.keras.Model)` creates a custom Keras model by inheriting from the base `tf.keras.Model` class. The base class already knows how to work with `compile(...)`, `fit(...)`, `evaluate(...)`, saving, and trainable variables. Your job is to fill in the model-specific parts:
- `__init__(...)` defines the layers and stores them as attributes.
- `call(...)` describes how one batch of inputs flows through those layers.

When you later call `model(x)` or use `model.fit(...)`, Keras automatically runs your overridden `call(...)` method behind the scenes. This is why the method name and its input/output behavior must match the Keras pattern shown in the documentation.

---
**Encoder-Decoder CNN**

In image classification, a CNN usually keeps reducing the image into smaller feature maps and finally predicts one class label. For image segmentation, this is not enough because we need a prediction for **every pixel**. Therefore, a common segmentation model uses an **encoder-decoder** structure.

The **encoder** is the first half of the model. It uses convolution and pooling layers to extract visual features from the image. As the feature maps become smaller, the model can capture larger patterns, such as the rough shape and position of the pet.

The **bottleneck** is the middle part of the model. It works on the smallest feature map and stores the most compressed representation of the input image.

The **decoder** is the second half of the model. It uses **upsampling** layers to increase the feature map size again, so the model can recover a pixel-level output. In this lab, the final output should return to `128 x 128`, matching the height and width of the target mask.

The final layer uses a `1 x 1` convolution with `sigmoid` activation. This means every pixel receives one probability value: close to `1` for pet foreground, and close to `0` for background.

---
**Suggested TensorFlow Components**

- `tf.keras.Model` : https://www.tensorflow.org/api_docs/python/tf/keras/Model
- `tf.keras.layers.Conv2D` : https://www.tensorflow.org/api_docs/python/tf/keras/layers/Conv2D
- `tf.keras.layers.MaxPooling2D` : https://www.tensorflow.org/api_docs/python/tf/keras/layers/MaxPooling2D
- `tf.keras.layers.UpSampling2D` : https://www.tensorflow.org/api_docs/python/tf/keras/layers/UpSampling2D

---
**Suggested Model Structures**

- **Simple:** Use a shallow encoder and decoder with a small number of filters. This is the easiest structure to implement and is useful for checking whether your data pipeline, mask output shape, loss, and metric are correct.
- **Deep:** Add more encoder and decoder layers while keeping the filter numbers moderate. This usually helps the model capture larger pet shapes because the bottleneck sees a wider receptive field.
- **Wide:** Increase the number of filters in each convolution layer. This gives the model more capacity, but it also trains more slowly and may overfit more easily.
- **Adjacent Convolutions:** Use two consecutive `Conv2D` layers with the same number of filters before each `MaxPooling2D`, and also use two consecutive `Conv2D` layers after each `UpSampling2D`. The model refines local image patterns before changing feature-map resolution.


A good starting point is the simple encoder-decoder. If the validation IoU is clearly underfitting, try making the model deeper first, then wider.
**Local model check:** `BackgroundSeparater()(tf.zeros((2,128,128,3)))` must have shape `(2,128,128,1)`. The reference architecture has 277,889 trainable parameters, but your parameter count may differ. Use the default constructor configuration consistently when saving and loading.

"""

